{"result": {"response": {"id": "gen-1787751029-d3dM8sshAka7q9HCZrYZ", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "{\n    \"title\": \"Structured Cabling: Horizontal Cabling Infrastructure\",\n    \"summary\": \"This community report details the 'Cabeamento Horizontal' (Horizontal Cabling) subsystem, a critical component of structured telecommunications infrastructure. It outlines its function, standards, components, and limitations. The horizontal cabling connects work areas to telecommunication closets, adhering to standards like ANSI/TIA-568. Key components include UTP cables, outlets, faceplates, and Keystone Jacks, with limitations on length (90 meters for permanent link, 100 meters total). Potential issues like Return Loss are also considered.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Definition and Role of Horizontal Cabling\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 'Cabeamento Horizontal' (Horizontal Cabling), also known as secondary cabling, is a fundamental subsystem within structured cabling. Its primary role is the final distribution of telecommunication services, connecting telecommunication closets (TC) or telecommunication rooms (TR) to user work area outlets. This cabling extends from the work area to the TC/TR, essentially bridging the gap between end-user devices and the core network infrastructure. It is the physical pathway that enables devices like computers and IP phones to connect to the local area network (LAN) [Data: Entities (63), Relationships (99, 392)].\\n\\nThe flexibility of horizontal cabling is a key advantage, allowing for easier adaptation to changes in office layouts and supporting various media types. However, its utility is also constrained by distance limitations and potential susceptibility to electromagnetic interference in the immediate work environment [Data: Entities (63)]. This subsystem is essential for establishing reliable network connectivity at the user level.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Adherence to Standards and Best Practices\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The design and implementation of horizontal cabling are governed by established industry standards, most notably ANSI/TIA-568. This standard provides a framework that ensures interoperability between components from different manufacturers and guarantees the overall performance and reliability of the cabling system [Data: Entities (361, 375), Relationships (378, 396)].\\n\\nKey aspects defined by ANSI/TIA-568 include specific requirements for cables, connectors, maximum distances, and network topologies. For instance, the standard mandates that the permanent link of the horizontal cabling should not exceed 90 meters, with the total channel length, including patch cords, capped at 100 meters for metallic cables [Data: Entities (376, 1501, 1502)]. Compliance with these standards is crucial for achieving certified performance levels and ensuring that the cabling can support current and future network demands.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Key Components of Horizontal Cabling\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The horizontal cabling system comprises several essential components that work together to facilitate network connectivity. These include the cabling itself, such as UTP (Unshielded Twisted Pair) or optical cables, which form the physical transmission medium [Data: Entities (824, 370, 369)].\\n\\nAt the termination points, 'Tomada (Telecommunication Outlet)' and 'Outlet' serve as the interfaces where devices connect to the network, commonly featuring RJ-45 female connectors [Data: Entities (386, 374, 424, 391), Relationships (422)]. These outlets are typically housed within 'Faceplates' for a clean finish and secure mounting [Data: Entities (387), Relationships (423)]. 'Keystone Jacks' are another critical hardware component, used for connecting cables within outlets or patch panels, including specialized types like SC 568 Duplex for optical cables [Data: Entities (395, 435), Relationships (434)]. Furthermore, 'Connecting Blocks' are used for cable interconnections, and 'Consolidation Points' (CP) offer flexibility for derivations in the cabling layout, although these differ from the TIA-568 approach [Data: Entities (389, 96), Relationships (140)]. Protective and organizational systems like 'Conduítes, Dutos e Canaletas' are also integral for managing and safeguarding the cables [Data: Entities (388), Relationships (424)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Length Limitations and Performance Metrics\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The performance and reliability of horizontal cabling are directly influenced by its physical length. Standards like ANSI/TIA-568 impose strict maximum length restrictions to ensure signal integrity. The 'Permanent Link,' which represents the fixed portion of the horizontal cabling from the outlet to the telecommunication closet, is limited to 90 meters [Data: Entities (376, 1502)].\\n\\nWhen considering the entire 'channel,' which includes patch cords at both ends, the total length for metallic cabling cannot exceed 100 meters [Data: Entities (1501)]. These limitations are critical for managing signal degradation and ensuring that data can be transmitted effectively without excessive loss or errors. 'Return Loss,' a measure of signal reflection caused by impedance mismatches, is a key parameter monitored during certification tests for horizontal cabling, as excessive return loss can lead to interference and communication problems [Data: Entities (379), Relationships (400)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Relationship with Backbone Cabling and Network Architecture\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Horizontal cabling does not exist in isolation; it is a crucial part of a larger network architecture. It serves as the 'secondary cabling,' connecting to the 'Backbone Cabling' (primary cabling) which provides the main routes for data transmission between different parts of a building or campus [Data: Entities (93), Relationships (137)].\\n\\nThe 'Backbone Cabling' is a fundamental type of 'Network Architecture' [Data: Entities (1143), Relationships (1753)]. Therefore, the effectiveness and efficiency of the horizontal cabling directly impact the overall performance of the network architecture. While horizontal cabling focuses on the final distribution to user areas, backbone cabling handles the higher-capacity, longer-distance connections that link telecommunication rooms and data centers.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 6.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate-high due to the foundational role of horizontal cabling in network infrastructure, where failures or non-compliance can significantly disrupt business operations and data integrity.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null, "reasoning": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "STOP"}}], "created": 1787751029, "model": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1387, "prompt_tokens": 4564, "total_tokens": 5951, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "image_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 3756, "video_tokens": 0}, "cost": 0.00067316, "is_byok": false, "cost_details": {"upstream_inference_cost": 0.00067316, "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.00011836, "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 0.0005548}}, "formatted_response": null, "provider": "Google", "content": "{\n    \"title\": \"Structured Cabling: Horizontal Cabling Infrastruc